13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 26–August 3, 1862","place":"Washington, D.C.; the Shenandoah Valley, Rappahannock line, and Fredericksburg in northern Virginia; Gordonsville and Richmond in central Virginia; and Harrison’s Landing on the James River","result":"The Lincoln administration consolidated three independent Federal commands as the Army of Virginia under Maj. Gen. John Pope. The new army could not immediately cooperate with George B. McClellan’s Army of the Potomac because Robert E. Lee’s army stood between them. Henry W. Halleck became general-in-chief, McClellan’s Peninsula army was ordered north, and Pope’s July addresses and orders marked a more coercive Federal policy toward Confederate property, guerrillas, and disloyal civilians","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"John Pope’s background, the creation of the Army of Virginia, the Federal command problem after the Seven Days, and the policy dispute between Pope and McClellan","note":"The narrative covers Pope’s earlier career before following events from his June appointment through the August 3 order withdrawing the Army of the Potomac from the Peninsula."},{"label":"John Pope in 1862","value":"Forty years old; West Point class of 1842","note":"Pope was born in Louisville, Kentucky, grew up in Illinois, served in the Topographical Engineers, and received Mexican War brevets for Monterrey and Buena Vista."},{"label":"Western record","value":"New Madrid, Island No. 10, and the advance on Corinth","note":"Pope’s Army of the Mississippi captured New Madrid in March and Island No. 10 in April 1862, then joined Halleck’s advance on Corinth. These operations established the reputation that brought Pope east."},{"label":"Army of Virginia created","value":"June 26, 1862","note":"Lincoln’s order combined the commands of John C. Frémont, Nathaniel P. Banks, and Irvin McDowell and assigned the new army to Pope."},{"label":"Army of Virginia mission","value":"Protect Washington and western Virginia, defeat Jackson and Ewell, threaten toward Charlottesville, and aid McClellan","note":"Those four requirements appear in Lincoln’s June 26 order. They imposed simultaneous defensive and offensive responsibilities on Pope’s new command."},{"label":"Initial strength and distribution","value":"About 45,000 men spread across northern Virginia","note":"The U.S. Army campaign summary gives approximately 45,000. The episode describes the three commands as occupying an arc of roughly 60 miles from the lower Shenandoah Valley to Fredericksburg."},{"label":"I Corps","value":"Mountain Department troops under Frémont, then Franz Sigel","note":"Frémont, who was senior to Pope, asked to be relieved rather than serve under him. Stanton selected Sigel as the replacement."},{"label":"II Corps","value":"Shenandoah Department troops under Nathaniel P. Banks","note":"Banks’s army had been defeated by Jackson at Front Royal and Winchester during the preceding Valley Campaign."},{"label":"III Corps","value":"Irvin McDowell’s Department of the Rappahannock troops","note":"Part of McDowell’s command under James Shields had been defeated at Port Republic. Troops retained inside Washington’s fortifications were excluded from the new corps."},{"label":"Pope’s first headquarters","value":"The War Department in Washington","note":"Pope initially directed concentration from the capital and followed the Seven Days through the War Department telegraph office. He did not join his field command until late July."},{"label":"McClellan at Harrison’s Landing","value":"Army of the Potomac on the James River from July 2","note":"The Seven Days ended with McClellan separated from Pope by Richmond and Lee’s Army of Northern Virginia. McClellan wanted reinforcements and a renewed Peninsula offensive."},{"label":"Pope’s strategic objection","value":"A direct march toward Richmond would expose his army to Lee before McClellan could support it","note":"Pope made this argument in his July 4 letter to McClellan. He proposed holding a line that protected Washington and denied easy Confederate access to the Shenandoah until a coordinated plan existed."},{"label":"New general-in-chief","value":"Henry W. Halleck, appointed July 11, 1862","note":"Lincoln restored the post that had been vacant since he removed McClellan from the general-in-chief role in March. Halleck visited Harrison’s Landing July 24–27 to assess McClellan’s position."},{"label":"Burnside and Army of the Potomac command","value":"The episode reports that Ambrose E. Burnside declined an offer in late July","note":"The hosts place the offer around July 25 and state that Burnside doubted his fitness for the larger command and was loyal to McClellan. The National Park Service confirms that Burnside declined an offer to replace McClellan during the summer crisis."},{"label":"Peninsula withdrawal order","value":"August 3, 1862","note":"Halleck ordered the Army of the Potomac transferred from the Peninsula toward Aquia Creek so it could join Pope and cover Washington."},{"label":"Army condition in late July","value":"Three corps needing concentration, refit, and resupply","note":"The episode also emphasizes morale and command friction. Pope used public language and orders to promote a more aggressive identity for the new army."},{"label":"Pope’s address to the army","value":"July 14, 1862","note":"The address contrasted Pope’s western service with eastern concern for defensive positions, supply bases, and retreat. The episode’s title comes from its opening claim that Pope had come from the West."},{"label":"Reaction inside the armies","value":"Some enlisted soldiers approved; many officers resented the criticism","note":"The episode describes the strongest hostility among Army of Virginia officers senior to Pope and among McClellan’s circle in the Army of the Potomac. Fitz John Porter condemned the address in a July 17 private letter."},{"label":"Administration role in the address","value":"Stanton influenced its content and Lincoln approved it in the episode’s account","note":"The hosts interpret the address as an administration-backed criticism of McClellan’s military approach, not simply an unsanctioned statement of Pope’s personality."},{"label":"General Orders No. 5","value":"Army to subsist from the country in its area of operations","note":"Pope permitted requisition of food and forage and distinguished receipts for loyal citizens from vouchers payable only after restoration of peace for those who supported the Confederacy."},{"label":"General Orders No. 7","value":"Collective penalties for guerrilla attacks and damage to communications","note":"The order held nearby civilians responsible for repairing railroads, roads, telegraphs, and bridges and authorized severe punishment for attacks made from civilian cover."},{"label":"General Orders No. 11","value":"Loyalty oaths, expulsion, and arrest of disloyal male civilians","note":"Men who took the oath and provided security could remain. Those who refused were to be sent beyond Federal lines and treated as spies if they returned."},{"label":"Policy purpose and effect","value":"Suppress guerrillas and shift the burdens of war onto active Confederate supporters","note":"The National Archives and National Park Service describe the orders as administration-approved measures that allowed executions, financial assessments, confiscation, requisition, and destruction of property. Their broad collective penalties also endangered civilians."},{"label":"McClellan’s opposing policy","value":"A limited war directed against Confederate armed forces rather than the Southern population","note":"McClellan’s July 7 Harrison’s Landing letter advised against subjugation, confiscation, and forcible abolition. His later instructions warned the Army of the Potomac against plunder."},{"label":"Second Confiscation Act","value":"Approved July 17, 1862","note":"The statute expanded Federal authority against rebel property and declared free enslaved people held by persons engaged in rebellion who came under United States control under the act’s terms."},{"label":"Confederate response","value":"Jackson sent toward Gordonsville on July 13","note":"Lee moved Jackson north to protect the Virginia Central Railroad junction and confront Pope while Longstreet remained near Richmond. The deployment deepened the danger created by the two separated Federal armies."},{"label":"Battle losses in this episode","value":"None","note":"This is an organizational, strategic, and policy episode. It reviews earlier battles and prepares the Northern Virginia Campaign but does not narrate a new battle."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“General John Pope: A Life for the Nation” by Peter Cozzens","note":"The official episode page and the podcast’s book list identify this biography as the recommendation."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Shenandoah, Irvin McDowell, Franz Sigel, George B. McClellan, Fitz John Porter, and Harrison’s Landing","note":"The automated transcript produces “Lower Chandoe,” “MacDowell,” “Siegel,” “Lomac,” “Fitzjohn,” and “Harrison’s landing.” “Lomac” refers to the hosts’ nickname “Little Mac” for McClellan."}],"maps":[{"title":"The separated Federal armies and the Army of Virginia’s formation","caption":"Lincoln combined the former Mountain, Shenandoah, and Rappahannock commands under Pope. McClellan remained at Harrison’s Landing, with Lee near Richmond and able to move toward Gordonsville between the two Federal armies. Rivers, routes, positions, arrows, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Lincoln’s June 26 order, National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep170-strategic-context.svg","alt":"Generalized schematic map showing the three corps of the Army of Virginia, Pope near Gordonsville, McClellan at Harrison’s Landing, and Lee near Richmond."},{"title":"Government-published map of the Army of Virginia’s theater","caption":"This 1866 map accompanied Pope’s report to the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War. It shows the roads, railroads, rivers, terrain, and communities across the July–August theater but does not plot troop movements.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division, from the 1866 supplemental report of the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep170-army-of-virginia-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of northern and central Virginia showing roads, railroads, rivers, and towns in the Army of Virginia’s field of operations."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:12","audioTime":72,"title":"The previous episode’s turning point is reviewed","detail":"The Seven Days and the failed Peninsula Campaign are linked to Lincoln’s decision to bring Pope and Halleck east."},{"time":"1:39","audioTime":99,"title":"The episode turns to John Pope","detail":"The narrative begins with Pope’s reputation and the circumstances behind his selection for the new Army of Virginia."},{"time":"1:44","audioTime":104,"title":"A correspondent describes Pope","detail":"An August 1862 interview portrays an energetic, confident, self-promoting officer whose strengths were paired with vanity and imprudence."},{"time":"2:33","audioTime":153,"title":"Pope’s age and origins are introduced","detail":"He was forty, born in Kentucky, and raised in Illinois when he took command in Virginia."},{"time":"2:43","audioTime":163,"title":"West Point and Mexico establish his professional record","detail":"Pope graduated in 1842 and earned brevets for Monterrey and Buena Vista during the Mexican War."},{"time":"3:13","audioTime":193,"title":"Professional ability and abrasive behavior are paired","detail":"The episode describes Pope as an expert horseman and capable officer with an impetuous, abrasive personality."},{"time":"3:19","audioTime":199,"title":"Pope enters Civil War volunteer service","detail":"He received a brigadier general’s commission in 1861 and served in Missouri before gaining an army command."},{"time":"3:26","audioTime":206,"title":"Pope receives the Army of the Mississippi","detail":"In February 1862 he took command of the Federal field army operating against Confederate positions on the Mississippi River."},{"time":"3:39","audioTime":219,"title":"New Madrid and Island No. 10 build Pope’s reputation","detail":"The successful campaign forced two important Confederate river positions to surrender in March and April."},{"time":"3:49","audioTime":229,"title":"Pope joins Halleck’s advance on Corinth","detail":"The Army of the Mississippi became part of Halleck’s large combined force after Shiloh, and Confederates evacuated Corinth."},{"time":"4:10","audioTime":250,"title":"Lincoln selects a western commander","detail":"Pope’s victories and lack of involvement in the near-disaster at Shiloh made him a prominent candidate for the Virginia assignment."},{"time":"4:38","audioTime":276,"title":"Politics also favor Pope","detail":"His Republican affiliation, support for abolition, and preference for aggressive war aligned him with the administration’s changing priorities."},{"time":"4:58","audioTime":298,"title":"Pope reaches Washington","detail":"He arrived on June 22 after being summoned from the Western Theater."},{"time":"5:02","audioTime":302,"title":"The Valley Campaign shapes the new assignment","detail":"Banks, Frémont, and part of McDowell’s command had each been defeated during Jackson’s operations in the Shenandoah Valley."},{"time":"5:29","audioTime":329,"title":"Stanton explains the consolidation","detail":"Three independent commands in northern Virginia are to become one army under Pope."},{"time":"5:41","audioTime":341,"title":"Gordonsville becomes the operational objective","detail":"A movement toward the Virginia Central Railroad junction was intended to pressure Richmond and support McClellan."},{"time":"5:51","audioTime":351,"title":"Pope receives a mixed welcome","detail":"Northern newspapers respond more favorably than soldiers and officers in the commands being placed under an unfamiliar general."},{"time":"6:01","audioTime":361,"title":"All three corps commanders outrank Pope","detail":"Frémont, Banks, and McDowell were senior in rank, creating immediate resentment within the new command structure."},{"time":"6:16","audioTime":376,"title":"Frémont asks to be relieved","detail":"He refuses the effective demotion to corps command under Pope and leaves active field service."},{"time":"6:35","audioTime":395,"title":"Stanton selects Franz Sigel","detail":"The secretary of war, rather than Pope, chooses Sigel to replace Frémont in command of I Corps."},{"time":"6:46","audioTime":407,"title":"McClellan learns of the new army","detail":"The Army of the Potomac commander had not been consulted about the creation of the Army of Virginia or Pope’s appointment."},{"time":"7:10","audioTime":431,"title":"Lee opens the Seven Days","detail":"The June 26 attack at Mechanicsville begins the campaign that drives McClellan away from Richmond as Pope’s appointment is announced."},{"time":"7:51","audioTime":472,"title":"Pope assumes command","detail":"He takes formal command on June 27, the day of Gaines’s Mill, and begins concentrating the scattered army."},{"time":"8:10","audioTime":490,"title":"The three corps span a wide arc","detail":"The episode places them from the lower Shenandoah Valley to Fredericksburg along the Rappahannock."},{"time":"8:18","audioTime":498,"title":"Pope remains at the War Department","detail":"He directs the army from Washington rather than immediately establishing headquarters with the troops."},{"time":"8:49","audioTime":529,"title":"The telegraph office becomes Pope’s observation post","detail":"From Washington he monitors McClellan’s battles and advises Lincoln as the president follows the campaign by telegraph."},{"time":"9:09","audioTime":549,"title":"Pope criticizes the retreat to the James","detail":"He argues that abandoning the White House Landing line allows Lee to stand between the two Federal armies."},{"time":"9:32","audioTime":572,"title":"Pope presents his concern to the cabinet","detail":"The episode states that he repeated the strategic objection at a June 28 cabinet meeting."},{"time":"9:40","audioTime":580,"title":"Harrison’s Landing sharpens the disagreement","detail":"After McClellan establishes the James River base on July 2, he asks for reinforcements and wants Pope to open a second front north of Richmond."},{"time":"10:11","audioTime":611,"title":"Pope writes to McClellan","detail":"His July 4 letter begins with a promise of cooperation and then explains why he cannot safely move directly on Richmond."},{"time":"10:33","audioTime":633,"title":"The Seven Days disrupt Pope’s original plan","detail":"Pope states that the recent operations have changed the circumstances under which he expected to aid the Peninsula advance."},{"time":"10:48","audioTime":648,"title":"Pope defines a defensive interim mission","detail":"He proposes guarding Washington and denying Confederate access to the Shenandoah rather than fighting Lee alone near Richmond."},{"time":"11:29","audioTime":689,"title":"The Federal strategy reaches an impasse","detail":"McClellan will not renew operations before receiving major reinforcements, while Pope will not advance without a workable cooperation plan."},{"time":"11:51","audioTime":712,"title":"Lincoln seeks a superior for both army commanders","detail":"The administration responds to the divided command by restoring the position of general-in-chief."},{"time":"12:04","audioTime":725,"title":"Halleck becomes general-in-chief","detail":"The former western department commander is brought to Washington to coordinate Pope and McClellan."},{"time":"12:27","audioTime":748,"title":"Halleck inspects Harrison’s Landing","detail":"He visits July 24–27 with authority to assess McClellan’s position and continued command."},{"time":"13:08","audioTime":788,"title":"Burnside declines the larger army","detail":"The episode reports that Burnside was offered the Army of the Potomac but doubted his ability and would not displace his friend McClellan."},{"time":"13:31","audioTime":811,"title":"McClellan’s reinforcement demand ends the Peninsula option","detail":"After Halleck considers renewed operations, McClellan again asks for a force Washington will not supply, and the army receives withdrawal orders."},{"time":"14:01","audioTime":842,"title":"Pope finally joins the army in the field","detail":"Halleck’s arrival removes Pope’s reason for remaining in Washington, although he is told to wait for McClellan’s troops before major offensive action."},{"time":"15:09","audioTime":910,"title":"The army waits on the Rapidan and Rappahannock","detail":"Pope holds while the administration expects the Peninsula transfer and the junction of the two Federal armies."},{"time":"15:16","audioTime":917,"title":"The new army needs refit and resupply","detail":"Each corps has logistical and organizational problems, and morale also requires attention."},{"time":"15:40","audioTime":940,"title":"Pope tries to impose an aggressive identity","detail":"He spends his first month promoting a forward-looking, offensive spirit within the army."},{"time":"15:48","audioTime":949,"title":"The July 14 address is introduced","detail":"The document defines Pope’s desired command culture and his relationship with eastern officers."},{"time":"16:15","audioTime":976,"title":"Pope contrasts western attack with eastern caution","detail":"He tells the army that he came from the West and dismisses repeated concern with strong positions, supply bases, and retreat routes."},{"time":"17:54","audioTime":1074,"title":"The address becomes a lasting liability","detail":"Its confident language is later remembered in light of Pope’s defeat, but the episode notes that many enlisted soldiers initially welcomed it."},{"time":"18:13","audioTime":1094,"title":"Army of Virginia officers resent the criticism","detail":"They read the address as an accusation that eastern commanders, rather than their soldiers, lacked determination."},{"time":"18:26","audioTime":1106,"title":"McClellan’s circle reacts more strongly","detail":"The deliberate references to positions, retreat, and supply are understood as a direct criticism of the Peninsula Campaign."},{"time":"18:50","audioTime":1130,"title":"Fitz John Porter condemns Pope","detail":"McClellan’s close ally responds to the address in a hostile July 17 private letter."},{"time":"19:12","audioTime":1153,"title":"The administration’s role is identified","detail":"The episode states that Stanton influenced the text and Lincoln sanctioned its criticism of McClellan."},{"time":"19:46","audioTime":1187,"title":"The failed Peninsula Campaign changes Federal policy","detail":"The narrative links McClellan’s retreat to a declining administration belief in conciliation as the central method for restoring the Union."},{"time":"20:14","audioTime":1215,"title":"John Sherman describes support for harder war","detail":"The Ohio senator’s letter to his brother reflects growing pressure for confiscation, emancipation, and more forceful prosecution of the rebellion."},{"time":"20:45","audioTime":1246,"title":"Pope issues coercive orders in Virginia","detail":"The episode summarizes requisition of property, penalties for guerrilla attacks, loyalty oaths, expulsion, and punishment of civilians who attacked Federal soldiers."},{"time":"21:20","audioTime":1280,"title":"The orders divide the Federal armies","detail":"Many enlisted soldiers welcome the harder policy, while McClellan opposes its treatment of property and civilians."},{"time":"21:37","audioTime":1297,"title":"McClellan issues contrary instructions","detail":"He warns against plunder and defines the conflict as a war against Confederate armed forces rather than the Southern population."},{"time":"22:13","audioTime":1334,"title":"The policy is traced above Pope","detail":"Because Lincoln approved the orders, the episode treats them as evidence of an administration shift rather than an independent outburst by one general."},{"time":"22:47","audioTime":1367,"title":"The Harrison’s Landing letter provides the contrast","detail":"McClellan’s July 7 defense of limited war is set against Pope’s orders and the Second Confiscation Act."},{"time":"23:31","audioTime":1411,"title":"McClellan anticipates Pope’s defeat","detail":"After learning that Lee sent Jackson against Pope, McClellan privately predicts that Pope will retreat or be beaten and learn the value of logistics and retreat routes."},{"time":"24:16","audioTime":1456,"title":"Peter Cozzens’s Pope biography is recommended","detail":"The episode closes with “General John Pope: A Life for the Nation,” a full biography of the controversial commander."}],"command":[{"side":"United States national and field command","color":"#315f91","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"Commander in chief","subordinates":"Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton; created the Army of Virginia, appointed Pope, and selected Halleck as general-in-chief"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"General-in-chief from July 11, 1862","subordinates":"Strategic superior to Pope and McClellan; assessed Harrison’s Landing and ordered the Peninsula withdrawal"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Pope","command":"Army of Virginia","subordinates":"I Corps: John C. Frémont, then Franz Sigel; II Corps: Nathaniel P. Banks; III Corps: Irvin McDowell; chief of staff: Col. George D. Ruggles"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac at Harrison’s Landing","subordinates":"Remained a separate army commander until Halleck ordered his force transferred north to join Pope"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"Department of North Carolina","subordinates":"The episode reports that he declined an offer to replace McClellan; part of his force later reinforced Pope"}]},{"side":"Confederate national and field command","color":"#9a4037","branches":[{"commander":"President Jefferson Davis","command":"Confederate commander in chief","subordinates":"National political and military authority over the forces defending Richmond and central Virginia"},{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Held the central position near Richmond between Pope and McClellan after the Seven Days"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Lee’s force sent toward Gordonsville","subordinates":"Moved north beginning July 13 to protect the railroad junction and oppose Pope; Maj. Gen. Richard S. Ewell served under him"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Lee’s remaining wing near Richmond","subordinates":"Stayed near the Confederate capital while Jackson moved toward Pope"}]}],"people":[{"name":"John Pope","role":"Western Theater commander selected to organize and lead the new Army of Virginia","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep170-john-pope.jpg"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"President who created the Army of Virginia, assigned its mission, appointed Halleck, and approved the harder-war measures discussed in the episode","side":"United States"},{"name":"Edwin M. Stanton","role":"Secretary of war who helped design the new command structure, selected Sigel, and supported Pope’s policy direction","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"Former western commander appointed general-in-chief to coordinate Pope and McClellan","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep170-henry-halleck.jpg"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander whose position at Harrison’s Landing and limited-war views conflicted with Pope’s strategy and policies","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep170-george-mcclellan.jpg"},{"name":"John C. Frémont","role":"Mountain Department commander who asked to be relieved rather than command I Corps under Pope","side":"United States"},{"name":"Franz Sigel","role":"Officer selected by Stanton to replace Frémont in command of I Corps","side":"United States"},{"name":"Nathaniel P. Banks","role":"Commander of II Corps, formed from the former Shenandoah Department troops","side":"United States"},{"name":"Irvin McDowell","role":"Commander of III Corps, formed from the field forces of the Department of the Rappahannock","side":"United States"},{"name":"Ambrose E. Burnside","role":"North Carolina department commander who declined the offer to replace McClellan described in the episode","side":"United States"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"McClellan ally and Army of the Potomac corps commander who privately condemned Pope’s July 14 address","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Sherman","role":"Ohio senator whose letter to William T. Sherman expressed growing support for confiscation, emancipation, and harder war","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander positioned between Pope and McClellan after the Seven Days","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate commander sent toward Gordonsville to protect the railroad and confront Pope","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Richard S. Ewell","role":"Confederate division commander named with Jackson in the mission assigned to Pope’s new army","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander who remained near Richmond when Lee sent Jackson north","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep170-strategic-context.svg","alt":"Schematic map of northern and central Virginia showing the three Army of Virginia corps, Washington, Gordonsville, Richmond, and Harrison’s Landing.","caption":"Pope’s new army combined forces from the Shenandoah Valley and the Rappahannock. McClellan remained on the James while Lee held the central position near Richmond. The map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Lincoln’s June 26 order, National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Library of Congress maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep170-army-of-virginia-map.jpg","alt":"Historical black-and-white map of northern and central Virginia roads, railroads, rivers, and towns.","caption":"This map of Pope’s July–August field of operations accompanied his 1866 report to Congress. It provides geographic detail but does not show troop positions or movements.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep170-john-pope.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of John Pope in United States Army uniform.","caption":"John Pope brought a record of success at New Madrid, Island No. 10, and Corinth to his new command in Virginia.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep170-henry-halleck.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of Henry W. Halleck in United States Army uniform.","caption":"Henry W. Halleck became general-in-chief in July 1862 and was responsible for coordinating the separated Federal armies.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep170-george-mcclellan.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of George B. McClellan with his wife, Ellen Mary Marcy McClellan.","caption":"George B. McClellan remained with the Army of the Potomac at Harrison’s Landing and opposed Pope’s strategic assumptions and coercive orders.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep170-virginia-refugees.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white photograph of Virginia civilians and wagons identified by the National Park Service as refugees in 1862.","caption":"The National Park Service uses this National Archives photograph to illustrate civilians affected by the expansion of military operations and Pope’s harder-war policies in Virginia.","source":"National Archives and Records Administration, presented by the National Park Service; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 170 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/10/30/civil-war-podcast-episode-170/"},{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast book recommendations","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"},{"label":"National Park Service: John Pope","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/john-pope.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Pushing Forward","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/pushing-forward.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: From the Peninsula to Maryland—Burnside’s role in summer 1862","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/from-the-peninsula-to-maryland-burnside-s-role-in-the-summer-of-1862.htm"},{"label":"National Archives: Abraham Lincoln and the Guerrillas","url":"https://www.archives.gov/publications/prologue/2010/spring/lincoln-and-guerrillas"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Civil War campaign summary","url":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Battle of Second Manassas staff ride","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo111054/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo111054.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln’s June 26 order constituting the Army of Virginia","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/order-constituting-the-army-virginia"},{"label":"Primary source collection: Official Records of the Union and Confederate Armies","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/03003452/"},{"label":"U.S. Statutes at Large: Second Confiscation Act, July 17, 1862","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-12/pdf/STATUTE-12-Pg589.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress: McClellan papers timeline","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/george-brinton-mcclellan-papers/articles-and-essays/timeline/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: July 7 Harrison’s Landing letter cover","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1685600/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: map of the Army of Virginia field of operations","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448505/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John Pope portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666476/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Henry W. Halleck portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666431/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: George B. McClellan and Ellen Marcy McClellan portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666451/"}]}}]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Government-published map of the Army of Virginia’s theater",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep170-army-of-virginia-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Government-published map of the Army of Virginia’s theater at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep170-army-of-virginia-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of northern and central Virginia showing roads, railroads, rivers, and towns in the Army of Virginia’s field of operations.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Government-published map of the Army of Virginia’s theater"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"This 1866 map accompanied Pope’s report to the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War. It shows the roads, railroads, rivers, terrain, and communities across the July–August theater but does not plot troop movements."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep170-army-of-virginia-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division, from the 1866 supplemental report of the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
